    "styleType": "kawaii",
    "id": "note-1793692777333",
    "title": "Lab Questions",
    "content": "Why we need to keep the gradient history ?\nFirst why we need gradient : \nThe training of neural network follows a couple of steps :\nWe start by forwarding where we make prediction . Then we calculate the loss to see how much the loss , are we making a good model for predection . Then we have backpropagation to calculate the gridiants then we update the weights using those gradients .\nFor PyTorch any calculation is saved in graph format . Which gives it ability to do backward on those operations and calcuate the gradients easily using the chain rule .\nThis is from PyTorch perspective , However if we define the function that save the gradients during the training like what we have done in the lab (I will share the code ) the purpose it to know if the neurons  are learning by monitoring the gradients ..\nWe have updated the gradiesnt based on diffrent metrics we have std which stands for standard deviation which checks the spread of gradient in other words how much the values differ from eachc other . We have also the L2 norm which helps us monitor the gradients are they getting too small or too large . So the purpose of saving gradients history is detect vanishing/exploding gradients and monitor gradient distribution\n\nWe also keep history of saturation , first of all what is saturation .Saturation means that a neuron's activation function reaches a region where its output changes very little, even when its input changes significantly.We check that by taking the derivative of the activation function  and we check if its value id close to 0 .\nFor saturation history , what are we exactly saving ? we are saving the percentage (or fraction) of neurons that are saturated in each layer at every training iteration or epoch. Saturation history helps us:\n- Identify layers with many saturated neurons.\n- Observe whether saturation increases or decreases during training.\n- Investigate potential vanishing-gradient problems.\n- Compare the behavior of different layers.",
    "notebookId": "nb-1792919649772",
    "color": "blush",
    "kawaiiSticker": "strawberry",
    "stage": "shape",
    "tags": [],
    "createdAt": "2026-11-03T07:59:37.333Z",
    "updatedAt": "2026-11-03T10:20:40.231Z",
    "codeBlock": {
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def update_grad_history(grad_hist, grads, metric=\"std\"):\n    \"\"\"Append a ...\n\n    Args:\n        ...\n\n    Notes:\n        - std: np.std(tensor)\n        - l2:  np.linalg.norm(tensor) / sqrt(tensor.size)  (scale-invariant)\n        - mean_abs: np.mean(np.abs(tensor))\n        - max_abs:  np.max(np.abs(tensor))\n    \"\"\"\n    def reduce_metric(arr):\n        if metric == \"std\":\n            return float(np.std(arr))\n        if metric == \"l2\":\n            return float(np.linalg.norm(arr) / np.sqrt(arr.size))\n        if metric == \"mean_abs\":\n            return float(np.mean(np.abs(arr)))\n        if metric == \"max_abs\":\n            return float(np.max(np.abs(arr)))\n        raise ValueError(\"Unknown metric: \" + str(metric))\n\n    for kind, per_layer in grad_hist.items():\n        for key in per_layer.keys():  # e.g., \"dZ3\", \"dW2\", \"db1\"\n            if key in grads:\n                val = reduce_metric(grads[key])\n                per_layer[key].append(val)\n\n"
    },
