    "id": "note-1793549709002",
    "title": "Lab 3",
    "content": "User-Centered Design is an approach to designing applications, websites, or systems by putting the needs of the users at the center of the design process. \nInstead of asking:\n\"What features do I want to build?\"\nYou ask:\n\"Who will use my application? What problems do they have? How can I make their experience easier?\"\n1. How did Airbnb's design changes impact both the user and the business?\nThink about Airbnb's two main users:\n- Guests: People looking for accommodation.\n- Hosts: People offering their properties for rent.\nAirbnb needed to make the experience easier for both.\nImpact on users:\n- Better search filters help guests find accommodation matching their needs.\n- High-quality photographs help guests understand what they are booking.\n- Reviews and ratings help users make informed decisions.\n- Clearer pricing helps avoid unpleasant surprises.\n- Simpler booking processes save time.\n- Easier property-listing tools help hosts manage their accommodations.\nImpact on the business:\n- More users complete their bookings.\n- More guests trust the platform.\n- More hosts are encouraged to list properties.\n- Better experiences encourage customers to return.\n- Increased customer satisfaction can improve Airbnb's reputation.\nThe important relationship is: Better user experience → More trust → More successful bookings → Potentially more revenue.\n2. Which UCD principles can you identify in Airbnb's approach?\nHere are five important principles:\nUCD principle\t                    Airbnb example\nUnderstanding users\t        Designing for both hosts and guests.\nUser Requirements\t           Collecting feedback and observing user difficulties.\nUsability\t                           Simplifying search and booking.\nAccessibility\t                       Making information and navigation easier to understand.\nIterative design\t               Continuously testing and improving the platform.\n3. What is the relationship between ROI and UX design?\nROI means Return on Investment.In UX design, it measures whether the financial benefits of improving the user experience justify the investment :\nImagine Airbnb invests $100,000 in improving its booking experience. As a result, the redesign generates $300,000 in additional financial benefits. ROI={300,000-100,000}/{100,000} * 100 so ROI=200% .This means the company gained $200,000 beyond its initial investment.\nUX improvements can generate ROI through:\n- Increased bookings and revenue.\n- Better customer retention.\n- Reduced customer-support costs.\n- Fewer usability problems.\n- Improved customer satisfaction.\nRemember: UX is not simply an expense for making an application attractive. It can be a strategic investment that improves business performance.\n1. What improvements do you notice compared to the old version?\nOlder design\t                                                     Redesigned experience\nMore basic visual layout\t                                      Cleaner, more modern layout\nLess organized information\t                              Better information hierarchy\nLess emphasis on host identity\t                          Host photographs became more visible\nLess refined listing presentation\t                          More attractive property photographs\nMore visual clutter\t                                              More whitespace and simpler typography\nIn general we are matching more with the real world .\nRecognition rather than recall : the user must not force his mind to remember stuff we need to make sure that when he sees the design he will be able directly to interact with it .\n2. How do these design decisions reflect UCD principles?\nDesign decision\t                         UCD principle\t                                 Why?\nBetter search filters\t                      User needs\t                             Helps users find suitable accommodation.\nClearer                                           listing information\t                 Usability\tReduces confusion.\nHost photographs\t                       Trust and transparency\t              Makes the experience feel more personal.\nVisible booking widget\t                Efficiency\t                                   Reduces unnecessary navigation.\nNeighborhood information\t       Context awareness\t                    Helps users understand their destination.\nConsistent visual design\t                Consistency\t                               Makes the platform easier to understand.\n\nNotice something important: these changes aren't just decorative.They solve actual problems that users might encounter when booking accommodation.\nPart 3. AI & Creativity in User-Centered Design : \nAI could personalize:\n- Accommodation recommendations.\n- Search results.\n- Travel suggestions.\n- Search filters.\n- User notifications.\nThe objective is to reduce the time users spend searching and help them discover relevant options.\n\n2. What are the possible risks or ethical concerns when applying AI in UX design?\nHere are five important risks:\nRisk\tExplanation\tAirbnb example\nPrivacy\tAI may analyze sensitive user information.\tAnalyzing travel history and personal preferences.\nBias\tAI recommendations may favor certain users or properties.\tSome accommodations might receive less visibility.\nLack of transparency\tUsers may not understand why AI recommends something.\tRecommending one property over another without explanation.\nOver-personalization\tToo much personalization can limit user choices.Showing only one type of accommodation.\nOver-reliance on AI\tDesigners might trust AI instead of listening to real users.Using AI-generated personas instead of conducting actual interviews.\n\nActivity 1 : \n*Why the user must be always involved in the UCD : \nUnderstanding context : the designer must understand who is the user and what he does and where he works \nSpecify user requirements : we have to understand the neeeds of the user to be able to apply diffrent features .\nDesign Solutions : by testing and evaluate the prototype .\nEvaluation : feedback .\nActivity 2 :",
