    "styleType": "classic",
    "id": "note-1792493772764",
    "title": "Lab 1",
    "content": "A signal is a way to carry information from one place to another.\nFor example, when you speak on the phone, your voice is converted into an electrical or digital signal, transmitted through a communication channel, and then reconstructed at the other end.\nWe can represent a signal as a wave:\n- The horizontal axis represents time.\n- The vertical axis represents the signal's amplitude (its strength or value).\nHere are two examples of signals:\nA signal can be affected while traveling through a communication channel.\nWe have 3 affects : \n1. Delay\n2. Noise\n3. Attenuation\nLet's understand each one.\nA. Delay\nDelay means that the signal takes time to reach its destination.Imagine that you send a signal at time t=0, but it arrives at the receiver a little later.For example, suppose the original signal is: x(t)=sin(2pift) ; If the channel introduces a delay of tau seconds, the received signal becomes: y(t)=x(t-tau) Therefore: y(t)=sin(2pif(t-tau)) Notice the difference: we replaced t with t-tau .This means the same signal arrives later.Important: An ideal delay does not change the signal's amplitude or frequency. It simply shifts the signal along the time axis.\n\nB.Attenuation\nAttenuation means that the signal loses strength as it travels through the channel.Imagine shouting to someone who is far away. Your voice becomes weaker as the distance increases.An electrical signal can also become weaker when traveling through a cable.It affects the amplitude not the frequency .\n\nC.Noise \nNoise is an unwanted signal that gets added to the original signal.Imagine that you are talking to a friend, but someone nearby is making a loud noise.Your friend hears both your voice and the unwanted sound.\nThe same thing can happen when transmitting electrical signals.Mathematically: y(t)=x(t)+n(t)  .\nIf all of these three affects happend at the same time the signal formula will be : y(t)=A x(t-tau)+n(t) .\n\nFourier Transform : \nThe Fourier transform allows us to analyze a signal in terms of its frequency components.\nFor example, consider a signal containing two sine waves: x(t)=sin(2pi 100t)+0.5sin(2pi 300t) . In the time domain, we see the combined waveform. In the frequency domain, we see two frequency components:\n- 100 Hz, with amplitude 1.\n- 300 Hz, with amplitude 0.5.\nThe Fourier transform reveals these components .\nSo, what are we transforming into what?\nWe transform a signal from its time-domain representation into its frequency-domain representation.\nFFT stands for Fast Fourier Transform:It is an efficient algorithm for calculating the Discrete Fourier Transform (DFT).",
